How JavaScript Runs:
One thread, one call stack, and two queues. The output order is not the order you wrote:

```js
console.log('1 sync');
setTimeout(() => console.log('4 macrotask'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3 microtask'));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('3b microtask'));
console.log('2 sync');

// 1 sync
// 2 sync
// 3 microtask
// 3b microtask
// 4 macrotask
```

Every microtask drains before the next macrotask, so a chain of promises can starve timers:

```js
const block = (ms) => {
  const end = Date.now() + ms;
  while (Date.now() < end) { /* spin */ }
};

setTimeout(() => console.log('timer ran after'), 0);
block(120);                       // the whole event loop is frozen for 120ms
console.log('sync work done');
// sync work done
// timer ran after
```

Node and the browser both expose a way to see the effect from the inside: `performance.now()` is high-resolution, and blocking for 120ms shows up as a 120ms gap between two timestamps with no other work in between.

Lexical Structure and Syntax:
The lexical grammar decides what is a token before the parser ever sees structure. These all print the same string, but only because of how the tokens fall:

```js
const n = 1_000_000;              // numeric separator
const b = 0b1010;                 // 10
const o = 0o17;                   // 15
const h = 0xff;                   // 255

console.log(n, b, o, h);
console.log('a'.length, '\u{1F600}'.length);  // 1 2 — code units, not characters
```

Automatic semicolon insertion does not insert a semicolon where you might hope:

```js
// WRONG — parses as return undefined;
function bad() {
  return
    { ok: true };
}

// RIGHT
function good() {
  return { ok: true };
}

console.log(bad(), good());    // undefined { ok: true }
```

Optional chaining and the nullish operator are lexical too, and they short-circuit the whole rest of the chain:

```js
const user = { profile: null };
console.log(user.profile?.name);            // undefined, not a crash
console.log(user.missing?.a.b.c);           // undefined
console.log(user.profile?.name ?? 'anon');  // 'anon'
```

Variables, Scope and Hoisting:
`var` hoists to the function and is initialised as `undefined`; `let` and `const` hoist to the block but stay uninitialised in the temporal dead zone:

```js
console.log(typeof a);   // 'function' — hoisted and callable
function a() {}

try {
  console.log(b);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.name); // ReferenceError
}
let b = 1;

console.log(typeof c);   // 'undefined' — var is initialised to undefined
var c = 2;
```

Block scope is what fixes the classic loop-closure bug:

```js
const withLet = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) withLet.push(() => i);
console.log(withLet.map((f) => f()));   // [0, 1, 2]

const withVar = [];
for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) withVar.push(() => j);
console.log(withVar.map((f) => f()));   // [3, 3, 3]
```

`const` prevents rebinding, not mutation — the object it points at is still mutable:

```js
const list = [1, 2];
list.push(3);
console.log(list);        // [1, 2, 3]
// list = [];             // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.
```
